              {
                "id": "AC-001",
                "isSatisfied": false,
                "text": "The vendor step expects the tarball file name npm pack derives from plugins/core/lib-node/package.json (the name with a scope's leading @ dropped and its / turned into -, then -<version>.tgz), refuses a manifest without a name, and fails the sync when npm pack reports a different file name, so renaming the package does not stop the sync (BUG-BUILD-010; proved by the build's pack-name infrastructure tests, which cite this criterion)."
              },
              {
                "id": "AC-002",
                "isSatisfied": false,
                "text": "After the vendor step copies the packed plugin core into a consumer's vendor/sharpninja-mcpserver-plugin-core.tgz and rewrites its package.json reference, it refreshes that consumer's package-lock.json through npm. For a consumer whose package.json already referenced that stable tarball name before the sync (mcpserver-cline-plugin, mcpserver-cline-v2-plugin and mcpserver-opencode-plugin), the lockfile entry of the dependency its package.json names for the vendored tarball records the new tarball's SHA-512 integrity, package.json and every other lockfile line stay unchanged, and npm ci then succeeds without EINTEGRITY, also when the tarball's own package name differs from that dependency name (the core packs as @qbrainai/qbrain-ai-plugin-core while those consumers depend on @sharpninja/mcpserver-plugin-core). A plain npm install --package-lock-only leaves the stale integrity, and installing the bare file: spec adds a second dependency under the tarball's package name, so neither satisfies this; nothing but npm writes the lockfile (triage-report-de98c84b and -f2684f90, BUG-BASELINE-012; PLAN-SESSIONTRIAGEFIX-001 E1; proved by the build's Node vendor lockfile infrastructure tests, a mock gate then bindings, which cite this criterion)."
              },
              {
                "id": "AC-003",
                "isSatisfied": false,
                "text": "SyncAgentPlugins' version bump changes a Node plugin's package.json and package-lock.json only through npm, versioning each plugin root that has a package.json with npm version <next> --no-git-tag-version --allow-same-version --ignore-scripts: package.json changes in its version line only, package-lock.json in its two version lines only, no other character is re-escaped (an em dash, &&, + and >= stay as written), the vendored core's integrity is unchanged, and git gets no commit or tag; the bump writes plugin.json manifests and .version itself and never writes package.json or package-lock.json (triage-report-de98c84b and -f2684f90, BUG-BASELINE-012, operator ruling 2026-10-10 \"Never edit an npm lockfile. It belongs to npm. If you want it changed, use npm.\"; PLAN-SESSIONTRIAGEFIX-001 E1; found in the C3 sync of 2026-10-15, where the bump re-serialized all three lockfiles; proved by the build's Node vendor lockfile infrastructure tests, a mock gate then bindings, and its build-target test of the plugin version bump plan, which cite this criterion)."
              }
            ],
            "area": "MCP",
            "title": "Node core vendor package uses a version-less stable file name",
            "updatedAt": "2026-11-10T13:29:14.7993833-06:00",
            "priority": "medium",
            "description": "SyncAgentPlugins SHALL vendor the packed Node plugin core into consumer repositories under the version-less stable file name sharpninja-mcpserver-plugin-core.tgz, and SHALL NOT embed a package version in any vendored tarball file name or in the constant that names it. Rationale, from triage-report-52e8098cd299475d9922098f00d818b6: Build.SyncAgentPlugins.cs:465 hard-coded sharpninja-mcpserver-plugin-core-0.1.0.tgz while package.json had moved to 0.2.0 after a breaking-change bump, so the three Node plugin repositories held tarballs whose file name claimed the pre-breaking-change surface while their content was the new package; the versioned name is also why the constant existed at all, since consumer package.json files reference the tarball path and a version bump would break the reference. The vendor step SHALL discover consumer vendor directories by matching any sharpninja-mcpserver-plugin-core*.tgz variant so first-run migration finds the legacy versioned files, copy the packed artifact to the stable name, delete superseded variants from the vendor directory, and rewrite the consumer package.json dependency reference file:vendor/sharpninja-mcpserver-plugin-core*.tgz to the stable name via a targeted in-place replacement that preserves the file's formatting. The step SHALL also assert that npm pack produced a tarball whose embedded version matches plugins/core/lib-node/package.json, so a silent pack-versus-manifest drift fails the sync instead of shipping.\n**Covered by:** FR: FR-MCP-143; TEST: TEST-MCP-194, TEST-MCP-REPL-040",
            "status": "pending",
